<p>As <a href="https://www.nytimes.com/2026/10/07/books/review/sleeping-beauties-david-byrne.html">Adam Grant writes in the&nbsp;</a><a href="https://www.nytimes.com/2026/10/07/books/review/sleeping-beauties-david-byrne.html"><em>New York Times Book</em> <em>Review</em></a>, the&nbsp;range of examples covered in <em>Sleeping Beauties</em> includes “how advocates reawakened Bach’s compositions and Vermeer’s paintings after one and two centuries of slumber; how technological advances rekindled theories of continental drift and black holes; how accidents resuscitated hand-washing and genetics; and how war renewed interest in the submarine <em>and The Great Gatsby</em>.” <a href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=UDRvESBHxZc">Making an appearance on&nbsp;<em>Late Night with Seth Meyers</em></a>, Byrne was asked if any of his own work has undergone a similar process of disinterest followed by rediscovery. “It’s kind of the opposite,” he replies. “There’s things that I’ve done where I go, ‘That one does not hold up that well at all’&nbsp;” — which, if the irony of cultural history continues to operate as usual, will be exactly the records the children of today’s Talking Heads fans put on most enthusiastically a few years down the line.</p>
